  "body": "An example of it is https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#dom-li-value, which states the following:\n\n> If the value attribute is present, user agents must parse it as an integer, in order to determine the attribute's value. If the attribute's value cannot be converted to a number, **the attribute must be treated as if it was absent**. The attribute has no default value. (emphasis added)\n\nHowever, most browsers have the following behaviour:\n\n``` javascript\nvar li = document.createElement(\"li\");\n// null\nli.setAttribute(\"value\", \"foo\");\n// null\nli.value\n// 0\nli.hasAttribute(\"value\")\n// true\nli.getAttribute(\"value\")\n// \"foo\"\n```\n\n`li.value === 0` is from the part of the spec [here](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/infrastructure.html#reflecting-content-attributes-in-idl-attributes:rules-for-parsing-integers). However, the \"value\" attribute here is not being treated as absent, since `hasAttribute` returns true and `getAttribute` returns the value set by code a few lines above.\n\nFurther discussion among my peers indicates that whenever the spec mentions that when an attribute is being treated as absent, it does not mean the attribute is being treated as completely absent; it is only considered as absent for the purpose of parsing.\n\nIs this the correct way of interpreting \"treat attribute value as absent\"? If so, should the wording be changed so that it's clearer to readers that attributes are being treated as absent only for the purpose of parsing?\n",
